{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_ac1204966274a34cf957a765c4997617986d37cc3b833bb16713af440c8f5c14", "title": "Sevastopol", "text": "Sevastopol or traditionally Sebastopol ( or ; Russian: Севасто́поль ; Crimean Tatar: Акъяр, Aqyar ; ) is a city located in the southwestern region of the Crimean Peninsula on the Black Sea. As a result of the 2014 Russian annexation, the city is administered as a federal city of the Russian Federation, though Ukraine and most of the UN member countries continue to regard Sevastopol as a city with special status within Ukraine.", "score": 18.992}, {"id": "hp_doc_30419885ce1e2d542003829d920d15e5f43b15dfcc976162d7d4fe678074df4b", "title": "MS Vereshchagino", "text": "MV \"Vereshchagino\" is a Ukrainian, former Soviet medium trawler (seiner to be exact), built in 1978 and later converted to coastal passenger shipping. It was one of 345 ships of \"Vasily Yakovenko\" (project 502EM) class, which was built in Kiev from 1971 to 2000. On April 24, 2000, \"Vereshchagino\" survived a direct hit by a P-35 Progress anti-ship missile fired by Russian Navy from Sevastopol. There were no fatalities, one seaman was wounded by splinters.", "score": 7.6275}, {"id": "hp_doc_3fc36f325c1fcf5bf20313f30fd3e2f348fa5114e8e851d9b7bc98e0c09dddf5", "title": "Respect to Mehmetçik Monument", "text": "The Respect to Mehmetçik Memorial (Turkish: \"Mehmetçiğe Saygı Anıtı\" ) is a monument in the Gallipoli peninsula, Turkey. Mehmetçik is a common name given to soldiers in a war, just like G.I..", "score": 7.2795}], "remaining_searches": 1}
